<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    S5, the Lane kernel. plans/0019-s5-lane-kernel.md.

    Throwaway, on S4's and S2's precedent: the value is entirely in the recorded numbers, and the
    last task deletes this directory and records the deleting commit's parent.

    Deliberately NOT added to Borough.slnx. S4 and S2 are both in it "for convenience", and the
    convenience has a price the corpus has already paid twice: a spike in the solution is a spike
    the whole-repository build has an opinion about. S5 is built by its own path, which is what
    tools/lane-run.sh does.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <RootNamespace>S5.Lanes</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      BenchmarkDotNet, on S4's shape. It is the *cross-check* here rather than the primary
      instrument, and the asymmetry is deliberate.

      S5's deliverable is a derived ratio — Vehicles per Tick per core, and from it Segments in
      15.6 ms — not a table of nanoseconds per operation. S2 established the precedent for that
      shape: a harness that times its own loops and prints the ratio, because the ratio is what a
      reader has to be able to check. But a self-timed loop is an instrument nobody has calibrated,
      and S4's BenchmarkDotNet jobs are the calibration that exists. So both run, over the *same*
      kernel code, and a disagreement between them is itself a reportable result.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="BenchmarkDotNet" Version="0.15.8" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The arithmetic substrate, compiled in by source. Same argument as S2.Routing.csproj, and here
      it is load-bearing rather than tidy: S5 exists because adr/0016's headline figure was measured
      on a floating-point engine, and the question is what that structure costs in *our* arithmetic.
      A spike that could name Borough.Core's tables would be able to drift into measuring something
      else; under this, Fixed, IntegerMath and Transcendental are the only things of ours that are
      present in the compilation at all, and reaching for anything else is a build error rather than
      a habit.
    -->
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Borough.Core\Arithmetic\*.cs"
             Link="Substrate\%(Filename)%(Extension)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The analysers, as a build-time input and never as a dependency — the substrate decision's
      other half, exactly as in S2.

      BOR0201 is the one that matters here and it is the whole point of the spike: a Lane kernel
      that computed one intermediate in double would be measuring Citybound's engine again. The
      rest come along and are all wanted in Lanes/, which models arithmetic the simulation will
      actually perform. Harness/ is exempted from three of them, by path, in .editorconfig.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Borough.Analysers\Borough.Analysers.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
